course: everyday
lesson: 290
title: Water, small injuries, hay fever, growing up and eyesight
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your body needs water to work well, so drink water through the day, about six to eight cups, and more when it is hot or you are running about.
explain: Small injuries need simple, correct care. A cut is washed with clean water, pressed with a clean cloth to stop bleeding and covered. A bruise is cooled with a cold pack wrapped in a cloth. A burn is cooled under cool running water for 20 minutes — not ice, not butter.
explain: Example: a girl in Melbourne touches the hot oven door. She runs her hand under the cool tap at once and calls her dad, who keeps it under the water for twenty minutes. The cooling stops the burn going deeper.
explain: Some problems are hidden. After a hard knock to the head you may feel fine, but an adult must know so they can watch you. If you cannot read the board, tell a teacher and a parent: you may need an eye test.
explain: Bodies are all different: children grow at their own pace, and growth spurts come at different ages.
fact: Your body needs water to work well; drink about six to eight cups a day, more when hot or active.
fact: A headache, a dry mouth or feeling dizzy on a hot day can mean your body needs water: drink water, rest in the shade and tell an adult.
fact: For a small cut or graze, wash it with clean water, press with a clean cloth to stop bleeding and cover it with a plaster or bandage.
fact: Cleaning a cut stops infection, and covering it keeps dirt out.
fact: For a bruise, put a cold pack wrapped in a cloth on it for a few minutes; cold reduces swelling and pain.
fact: If you hit your head hard, tell an adult even if you feel fine, because a head injury needs watching.
fact: Cool a burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell an adult; cooling stops the burn going deeper.
fact: Hay fever is caused by pollen and brings sneezing and itchy eyes.
fact: With hay fever, tell a parent, keep windows closed on high pollen days and wash your face after being outside.
fact: Everyone grows at their own pace, and growth spurts come at different ages.
fact: If you cannot see the board at school, tell a teacher and a parent, because you may need an eye test.
exercise: You feel thirsty after football. What is the best drink? | Water | Fizzy cola | A big glass of syrup | Nothing until dinner
exercise: About how much water should a child drink each day? | About six to eight cups, more when hot or active | Exactly one cup a day | Only when it is very hot outside | Twenty cups or more every single day, however you feel
exercise: You scrape your knee on the playground. What should you do first? | Wash it with clean water | Rub soil on it to stop the blood | Leave it open and dirty to dry | Lick it clean
exercise: A small cut is bleeding. How do you stop it? | Press on it with a clean cloth | Shake your hand hard | Blow on it hard until it stops bleeding | Put butter on it
exercise: Why do we cover a cleaned cut with a plaster? | To keep dirt out | To make the cut heal in one minute | To hide it from your parents | To make it bleed more
exercise: You bump your leg on a table and a bruise appears. What helps? | A cold pack wrapped in a cloth for a few minutes | A hot water bottle pressed straight onto the skin for an hour | Pressing hard on it for an hour | Rubbing it with sand
exercise: Why does a cold pack help a bruise? | Cold reduces swelling and pain | Cold makes the bruise change colour at once | Cold kills the bruise | Cold makes the skin thicker
exercise: You bang your head hard on the climbing frame but feel fine. What should you do? | Tell an adult so they can watch you | Say nothing, because you feel fine | Go straight back to climbing higher | Wait a week and see what happens
exercise: You touch a hot iron and burn your finger. What should you do? | Cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell an adult | Put butter on it | Hold an ice cube on it for an hour, then rub some butter on it as well | Pop the blister and cover it
exercise: Why do we cool a burn under running water? | Cooling stops the burn going deeper | Water makes the burn hurt more so it heals | It washes the burn's colour away | Water makes the skin grow back at once
exercise: In spring a boy keeps sneezing and his eyes itch outdoors. What is the likely cause? | Hay fever caused by pollen | A cold caught from flowers | Eating too much fruit | Watching too much television
exercise: What helps on high pollen days? | Keep windows closed and wash your face after being outside | Open all the windows wide to let the fresh pollen-filled air come in | Roll in the long grass | Sleep with flowers by your bed
exercise: Your friends are all taller than you. What is true? | Everyone grows at their own pace, and growth spurts come at different ages | Something must be wrong with you, and you will always stay the shortest in class | You will always be the shortest | Only tall children grow up healthy
exercise: You cannot read the words on the board at school. What should you do? | Tell your teacher and a parent | Pretend you can see them and keep quiet | Squint and say nothing | Copy from a friend forever
exercise: Why should a child who cannot see the board tell an adult? | They may need an eye test and maybe glasses | Their eyes will fix themselves by tomorrow if they rest a bit | Teachers will give them easier work | It means the board is broken
test: A boy in Texas has been playing outside on a hot day and has a headache and dry mouth. What should he do first? | Drink water and rest in the shade | Run another lap to sweat it out of his body | Drink only fizzy drinks | Eat a bag of salty crisps
test: A girl falls off her bike and grazes her elbow. Put the right steps in order. | Wash it, press with a clean cloth, then cover it | Cover it at once without washing | Rub it with grass and leaves, then cover it up tight | Leave it, then wash it next week
test: Which of these is true about burns? | Cool running water for 20 minutes helps | Butter or oil is the best thing to put on a burn | Ice straight on a burn is best | A burn needs no cooling at all
test: A boy in Glasgow knocks his head hard in PE and says, "I'm fine, don't tell anyone." What should his friend do? | Tell a teacher, because a head injury needs watching | Keep the secret as he asked | Tell his parents next month | Make him do more PE to test whether he is really fine
test: Your sister bruises her shin on a chair. Which is the best help? | A bag of frozen peas wrapped in a tea towel for a few minutes | A bag of frozen peas straight on the skin for an hour | A hot bath with very hot water to warm up the bruise | Nothing, bruises are dangerous
test: A girl sneezes all through June whenever she plays in the park. What could her family do? | See if it is hay fever and close windows on high pollen days | Keep her indoors all summer long with the windows wide open | Let her pick armfuls of grass | Give her a hot drink and ignore it
test: Two ten-year-old twins are 8 centimetres apart in height. What is the best explanation? | People grow at their own pace, even twins | One twin is unhealthy | The shorter twin will never grow again after the age of ten | The taller twin must be older
test: A boy keeps getting headaches in class and sits at the front to read the board. What might help? | Telling a teacher and parent and having an eye test | Sitting right at the back of the class instead | Closing one eye all day | Not looking at the board at all
test: grazed my knee at the park, its bleeding a bit | Wash it, press with a clean cloth, then a plaster | Leave it open so the air dries it out by itself | Rub some dirt on to stop the blood | Put butter on it and wait
test: burnt my finger on the pan, my sis says put ice on it. right? | No, cool it under running water for 20 minutes | Yes, ice works best on burns | Yes, or rub some butter on it to cool it down | No, wrap it tight in a towel
test: bonked my head on the bunk bed but im fine lol | Tell an adult anyway, a head bump needs watching | Fine, if it doesn't bleed it's nothing | Just go to sleep and rest it off, you'll be fine | Only tell someone if it swells
test: cant read the board from the back, its all blurry | Tell your teacher and parent, you may need an eye test | Squint harder until it gets clearer, it'll go away on its own | Copy from the kid next to you | It's normal, everyone sees it blurry
explain: A boy in Scotland bumped his head hard on a climbing frame, felt fine and said nothing; that evening he was sleepy and sick, and at hospital he was found to have a head injury.
explain: A girl in Texas put butter on a burn from a hot pan, as an old saying wrongly tells people to; the burn stayed hot and got worse, and it took weeks to heal.
fact: After a head bump, signs like sleepiness, being sick or a bad headache can appear hours later and need a doctor.
fact: Butter, oil and toothpaste keep the heat in a burn; cool running water for 20 minutes is what helps.
test: banged my head on the goalpost but im fine | Tell an adult anyway, a head injury can show later | It's fine if you can still walk and run about | Only tell if it bleeds | Just sleep it off
test: nan says put butter on a burn | No, cool it under running water for 20 minutes | Yes, butter cools it down | Yes, then wrap it up tightly in a bandage | Only if it's a small burn
test: hot day, played footy 2 hours, didnt drink anything, dizzy now | Rest in the shade, drink water and tell an adult | Keep playing, it will pass if you just keep going | Drink a fizzy drink and run | Lie in the sun to warm up
test: did u hear a girl burnt her hand on the oven and her nan put butter on it and "it got way worse". lesson? | Cool a burn under cool running water for 20 minutes | Butter is best for burns | Toothpaste stops burns hurting | Put ice straight on it for an hour and keep it there
test: my mate says a boy bumped his head at footy, said he was fine and "went to sleep and never woke up". what's the real lesson | Tell an adult after a hard head bump, even if you feel fine | Never go to sleep for a whole week after a bump on the head | Head bumps are nothing to worry about | Only bumps that bleed matter
test: kids say Josh is "a titch, he'll never grow". pass it on? | No, everyone grows at their own pace; that's unkind | Yes, it's just true | Yes, and measure him in front of everyone at lunch | Yes, he'll find it funny
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the correct first aid for a burn? | Cool running water for twenty minutes, and an adult informed | Butter or oil applied at once | Toothpaste spread thickly over it and left to dry | Ice held directly upon the skin
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person who has struck their head hard should inform an adult even if they feel well. | Signs of injury may appear hours later and need a doctor | A head injury is always visible at once, so one would know already | Adults must record every injury for school | It prevents any bruise from forming
exam: It is given that a pupil cannot read the board in the classroom. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | The pupil shall inform a teacher and parent, since an eye test may be needed | The pupil shall copy the work of a neighbour for the rest of the year | The pupil shall squint until the eyes adjust | The pupil shall sit further from the board
exam: State the cause of hay fever. | Pollen, which brings sneezing and itchy eyes | Hay eaten by the sufferer, which irritates the nose | A fever caused by hot weather | Germs passed on by a sneeze
